  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1250 PM MDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nShowers and thunderstorms have continued today as one \nof more waves spin north across Idaho ahead of the low centered over \nnorthern California. Showers and storms already this morning have \nproduced moderate to heavy rainfall, as moisture continues to become \ndeeper and more conducive for this to happen. For the rest of today \nand into this evening, more showers and storms will \ndevelop...especially where the dry slot behind this morning's \nprecipitation has allowed for more sunshine to interact with the \nhumid airmass in place. Heavy rainfall at times is possible and will \nbe the main impact. Some stronger storms will produce hail and gusty \nwinds over 40 mph. The latter is already evident due to outflow from \nstorms over the eastern/southeast highlands, creating a slow moving \noutflow boundary across the Snake Plain and into portions of the \nMagic Valley. It is likely this boundary will become an additional \nfocus for storm development, in addition to anything from pure \nheating. There is a MARGINAL RISK for severe storms across the area, \nand certainly is possible with the conditions setting up. \n\nThe overall pattern remains unchanged through Saturday, as the \ncutoff low over California is finally picked back up by the steering \nflow across the rest of the western U.S. The low will still be a \nslow mover, and finally shifts over the state on Saturday. Until \nthat low passes, expect multiple rounds of showers and storms \nproducing heavy rainfall at times. A combination of much higher \nmoisture content and more widespread showers and storms tomorrow \ninto Saturday, the potential for higher rainfall amounts has jumped \nup quite a bit. Will everyone see the heaviest rainfall in the \nforecast? No, but the potential is there and higher than the past \nday or so. From the Wood River Valley south across the Magic Valley \nand into the South Hills/Albion Mountains, potential rainfall \namounts through Saturday afternoon are the \"lowest\" and up to 0.35 \nis possible. Elsewhere, the potential is there from 0.50-1.0\" with \nlocally higher amounts. The current storm total forecast is likely \nshowing the higher end of the forecast ranges, as likely not every \nsquare mile shown in the gridded forecast is going to see those \namounts. We considered Flash Flood Watches especially in areas with \nburn scars, but for now are opting to handle it on a case-by-case \nbasis in terms of any needed warnings. \n\nWraparound showers and storms are possible Sunday. Heading into next \nweek, there is a struggle on what happens with the next low moving \ninto the western U.S. Before it appeared the low would stay the \nsouth and we would remain dry. There is some indication it may now \ndrift far enough north to bring some clouds and potentially more \nshowers and storms.  That seems to be handled by the Blend of Models \nwhich does have isolated coverage daily across portions of central \nand eastern Idaho. Temperatures will struggle to hit the 70s \ntomorrow and Saturday, before warming back up early next week.",
